  "doc": "City south faces under an angled view (night n8 lane city, FacadeArt / ChunkLift): under a tilt every building's roof is lifted by its height (ViewMode.lift_px(height_m * height_k)) and its south face is drawn under it, tiled bay by bay across the footprint's width from a style's wall strip. by_name: a city_textures `name` -> its style and height in metres (else default). height_k scales every height (1 = true height; the owner's call: a shortened cut for play visibility). styles: art = the strip per view art key (`<art>` is the view row's art, p55 / p70), bays = bay cells in the strip, bay_m = a bay's width on the ground. art_fallback: an art key whose strip is missing draws the named key's strip (p70 draws the p55 drawing, stretched by the geometry, until the 70 twins land). occlude: a body behind a building draws as an ink silhouette under the redrawn face and roof (CityOcclusion; false = the body draws over the lifted roof). parked_cards: hand the kerb-parked cars to VehicleParked as cards instead of drawing them flat, per chunk only while VehicleParked.drawn() (a live VehicleView: the arena city has none and keeps its flat cars). `top` draws none of this (bit-identical). props: the street props standing as cards under a tilt (PropCards, five facings cut by tools/art/city_prop_cut.py from the library's prop sheets, p55 / p70); a name not listed (fire_escape) stays flat; lamp_post and traffic_light stand at their post base (foot_m) with the arm turned by the flat art's turn; remove a row to put a prop back flat. storeys (optional per style, by art key): the wall stacked from whole storeys at the elevation's aspect (FacadeStoreys, tools/art/city_facade_storeys.py), the `art` sheet holding an upper row above a ground row, ground_h / upper_h = the rows' px, bay_m = the storey-wall's own bay width; a style without a row for this art key keeps the single stretched strip (`art`, p55). cast_m (n10 world): a pole's ground shadow stays a short contact dash (0.7 m, no blob): the old 1.6-1.8 m stroke with a globe at its end read as the post lying flat on the pavement.",
